nodecode-gogogoogle is a rewrite of josefacero's josefacero/hermes-gogogoogle:

  https://github.com/josefacero/hermes-gogogoogle
  commit f293a64fab681b693fa9239c37258bc18b1552ae

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Josef Acero (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin ran the Antigravity `agy` CLI as a worker on the
user's own Antigravity plan, checked the working tree with git, and had a fresh
model verify the result. All of that is here in Common Lisp: the same three
tools under the same names and arguments (antigravity_delegate,
antigravity_job, antigravity_stats) answering the same JSON fields; the same
roles and briefs; the same session permission rules for the read-only roles
(every MCP server except the browser denied, the Playwright tools that run
code, type or upload files denied, the safe ones explicitly allowed); the same
workdir guard (a drive root, the home folder, a credential folder and every
blocked root refused, inside or containing); the same git fingerprint before
and after, the same READ-ONLY VIOLATION, the same verifier chain with its one
valid first-line verdict and its quota cooldowns; the same run log; and
/gogogoogle for the operator to read it. The process the plugin started is the
same one, with the prompt on its standard input, `--output-format stream-json`
on its output, `--dangerously-skip-permissions` for a `do` run and
`--settings` permission rules for the others, and a child environment stripped
of provider keys.

What was left out, and why: Hermes's `pre_tool_call` approval hook, because
Nodecode shows the operator the tool call itself before it runs, so the
plugin's second gate would ask the same question twice; Hermes's
`contextvars` copy for a background thread, because the cell carries the
running settings and its two seams into the job thread instead; the
`GOGOGOOGLE_BROWSER`, `ANTIGRAVITY_MODEL`, `ANTIGRAVITY_VERIFIER_MODELS` and
`GOGOGOOGLE_BLOCKED_ROOTS` environment fallbacks, because a cell's section is
the one place its settings live; the Windows `taskkill` tree kill, because the
bounded wait ends the call either way; and the browser snapshot cleanup is
reported but not performed, as before. The job thread, the run log and the
cooldown table now live in the cell's own data folder under the Nodecode home
rather than in Hermes's plugin data folder.
